+`ENTRY_POINT` is a protocol-defined distinguished caller address used for `DEFAULT` and `VERIFY` frames. It is not specified as a deployed contract or precompile, and contracts MUST NOT assume anything about its code, balance, or caller type beyond address equality. Contracts called from `DEFAULT` or `VERIFY` frames therefore observe `CALLER = ENTRY_POINT` and `CALLVALUE = 0`, so heuristics that infer EOA-or-contract properties from `CALLER` may not behave as expected. Its numeric equality with the `APPROVE` opcode value has no semantic significance.
+
+`AUTH_MANAGER` is a reserved system contract address holding registered signer entries and per-signer nonce streams. Its address (`0xTBD`) and code hash are finalized at activation; the runtime semantics live in the [AuthManager reference contract](../assets/eip-8141/AuthManager.sol). System-only entry points (`advanceNonce`) are gated by `msg.sender == 0xfffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffe`, following the EIP-4788 / EIP-2935 system-call caller convention.
+
+**Stream-registration invariant**: a non-zero `tx.signer` is valid only if a signer entry exists at `(tx.sender, tx.signer)` in `AUTH_MANAGER`. Pre-frame consensus enforces this through `AUTH_MANAGER.checkNonce`, which returns `false` when the signer entry is empty. There are no signer-less non-zero streams; every active stream is tied to a registered signer.
+
+**Reference-implementation status**: the [AuthManager reference contract](../assets/eip-8141/AuthManager.sol) defines the canonical observable semantics (entry points, ordering, errors, events). Clients MAY implement equivalent native behavior outside EVM bytecode for performance; whichever shape ships is code-hash pinned at activation. The Solidity dynamic-`bytes` storage layout is illustrative: clients that pin the contract code itself MUST charge witness and access cost as for ordinary `bytes` storage; clients that implement native behavior MUST publish their effective per-pubkey slot count and access-cost rule.
