+#### Behavior
+
+The behavior of `APPROVE` is defined as follows:
+
+- If `ADDRESS != resolved_target`, revert.
+- Ensure that `scope` is one of the caller's allowed scopes in `frame.flags`, otherwise revert.
+    - To check this, evaluate that `scope != 0` and `scope & ~(frame.flags & APPROVE_SCOPE_MASK) == 0`.
+- Depending on the value of `scope`, perform the following:
+    - For `APPROVE_EXECUTION`: 
+        - If `sender_approved == true`, revert the frame.
+        - If `resolved_target != tx.sender`, revert the frame.
+        - Set `sender_approved = true`.
+    - For `APPROVE_PAYMENT`
+        - If `payer_approved` was already set, revert the frame.
+        - If `resolved_target` has insufficient balance, revert the frame.
+        - If `sender_approved == false` and `guarantor_approved == false`, revert the frame.
+        - Set `payer = resolved_target`, collect the transaction's maximum cost from `payer`, and set `payer_approved = true`.
+    - For `APPROVE_EXECUTION_AND_PAYMENT`: 
+        - If `sender_approved` or `payer_approved` was already set, revert the frame.
+        - If `resolved_target` != `tx.sender`, revert the frame.
+        - If `resolved_target` has insufficient balance, revert the frame.
+        - Set `sender_approved = true`, set `payer = resolved_target`, collect the transaction's maximum cost (`TXPARAM(0x06)`) from `payer`, and set `payer_approved = true`.
+    - For `APPROVE_GUARANTEE`:
+        - If `guarantor_approved` was already set, revert the frame.
+        - If `resolved_target` has insufficient balance to cover the transaction's maximum cost (`TXPARAM(0x06)`), revert the frame.
+        - Set `guarantor = resolved_target` and `guarantor_approved = true`. The guarantor's reservation is released and re-collected from the payer if a later `APPROVE_PAYMENT` overrides it; see [Guarantors](#guarantors).
+
+`APPROVE` does **not** advance the sender's nonce stream. Nonce consumption is a single, post-frame action (see [Behavior](#behavior) and [Flexible Nonces](#flexible-nonces)) that runs exactly once on successful inclusion and is outside frame rollback (including atomic-batch rollback). This single-point rule keeps replay protection consistent across the payer-paid, guarantor-paid, and sender-VERIFY-failed paths.
