+| 4     | Sender      | Target addr         | 0     | APPROVE_SCOPE_NONE   | Call data                | SENDER  |
+
+- Frame 0: Guarantor commits via `APPROVE(APPROVE_GUARANTEE)`. The signature binds `TXPARAM(0x0B)`, which covers Frame 1's signature data so the guarantor authorizes the specific sender it expects.
+- Frame 1: Sender authentication; if it fails, the guarantor pays.
+- Frame 2: `bumpNonce` advances the canonical paymaster's per-sender guarantor nonce, enforced structurally by the canonical paymaster runtime.
+- Frame 3: Sender repays the paymaster in ERC-20 (atomic-batched with frame 4 in some flows).
+- Frame 4: User's intended call.
+
+This pattern lets a public-mempool node accept the tx without simulating frame 1: the canonical guarantor in frame 0 plus the structural `bumpNonce` in frame 2 are enough.
+
+#### Example 6: PQ account using `permit`
+
+| Frame | Caller      | Target        | Value | Flags                         | Data                  | Mode   |
+| ----- | ----------- | ------------- | ----- | ----------------------------- | --------------------- | ------ |
+| 0     | ENTRY_POINT | Null(sender)  | 0     | APPROVE_EXECUTION             | PQ sigtype + sig hash | VERIFY |
+| 1     | ENTRY_POINT | Null(sender)  | 0     | APPROVE_EXECUTION_AND_PAYMENT | PQ sigtype + permit   | VERIFY |
+| 2     | Sender      | ERC-2612      | 0     | APPROVE_SCOPE_NONE            | permit(...)           | SENDER |
+| 3     | Sender      | DEX           | 0     | APPROVE_SCOPE_NONE            | swap(...)             | SENDER |
+
+- Frame 0: Sender authenticates the tx itself via a PQ scheme; the verifier resolves `(scheme, pubkey)` from `AUTH_MANAGER.getSigner(sender, tx.signer)`.
+- Frame 1: A second sender VERIFY frame binds the ERC-2612 `permit` digest into `verified_signers` via the same registered signer; the binding uses `TXPARAM(0x08)` for `tx.signer != 0` flows or any application digest the wallet computes.
+- Frame 2: `permit(...)` calls `ecrecover` internally; the modified `ECRECOVER` finds the bound `(digest, address)` and returns the sender's address without a secp256k1 recovery.
+- Frame 3: User's swap, now legal because `permit` succeeded.
